{
	/*
	Site-wide prop-for-that integration point — included once in Base.astro.

	1. Registers the declarative `auto` entry (see the <script> below). From here on, any
	   element anywhere in the site can opt into a live JS→CSS source just by adding
	   `data-props-for="<key>"` (and `data-props-to="<selector>"` to hoist the write to an
	   ancestor) — no further JS wiring needed. Plugin sources beyond the core
	   viewport/size/visibility/range set are lazy-loaded on first reference. Full source/plugin
	   catalog: node_modules/prop-for-that/README.md (or
	   https://github.com/argyleink/prop-for-that).

	2. Provides one shared, invisible, full-viewport pointer-tracking surface (below), hoisted
	   onto `<body>`. `pointerLocal` maths the cursor's position against *its bound element's*
	   `getBoundingClientRect()` — bind it to something document-sized (`<body>`, `<html>`) and
	   the ratio gets compressed/inaccurate on any page taller than one screen, since the box
	   being measured is the whole scrollable document, not what's actually on screen. A
	   `position: fixed; inset: 0` div's rect *is* the viewport, exactly, at all scroll
	   positions — so this element exists purely to give `pointerLocal` a correctly-sized
	   surface to measure against; `data-props-to="body"` is what makes the properties it writes
	   actually reach the rest of the page (custom properties only inherit to descendants, and
	   this div is a sibling of the real content, not an ancestor of it — see prop-for-that's own
	   "Custom properties inherit downward only" gotcha).

	   Result: `--live-local-pointer-x-ratio`/`-y-ratio` (0–1 across the whole viewport) and
	   `--live-local-pointer-inside` (1/0, cursor anywhere in the browser window) are available
	   from `<body>`'s inherited custom properties anywhere on the page. The header logo's
	   cursor-tracking glance (`logo.css`) is the first consumer; any future feature that wants
	   "where's the cursor, roughly" can read the same three properties instead of standing up
	   its own tracking element.

	   `pointer-events: none` is safe here specifically because `pointerLocal` listens on
	   `window` and only uses the element for its rect — it doesn't need real pointer events
	   delivered to this div itself (confirmed by reading prop-for-that's source directly, not
	   assumed).

	3. Registers one custom source, pointer-idle, using prop-for-that's own public register()
	   extension API rather than raw addEventListener/setProperty. There's no built-in plugin for
	   "how long since the pointer last moved" (checked the plugin catalog directly), and CSS
	   itself has no primitive for "time since a value last changed", so this is the smallest
	   piece of custom JS that still routes through the same batched writer as everything else
	   here. It writes --live-idle (1/0) onto :root, flipping to 1 after 3s with no pointermove
	   anywhere on the page and back to 0 the instant one arrives. .logo-mark reads it as the
	   trigger for logo.css's cursor-tracking container query, replacing an earlier, narrower
	   --live-local-pointer-inside-based trigger that only fired when the cursor physically left
	   the browser window, not when it just stopped moving (see logo.css for the full story).

	   Deliberately not gated by --live-local-pointer-inside here: with no more pointermove events
	   arriving once the cursor leaves the window, the idle timer already fires on its own within
	   3s, so one signal covers both cases, at the cost of up to a 3s lag specifically on the
	   window-leave case, which is an acceptable trade for a decorative idle state.

	4. Registers a second custom source, logo-pointer, bound directly to the header's .logo-mark
	   element (not hoisted — its own descendants, #Eye1Track/#Eye2Track/#MuzzleTrack/etc., are
	   inside it, so they inherit its inline-written properties for free). This exists because the
	   viewport-wide --live-local-pointer-*-ratio above goes practically flat once the cursor is
	   near the mark itself: those ratios span the whole viewport, but the mark is maybe 70px
	   across, so moving the real cursor all the way across the bear's own face barely nudges the
	   ratio (measured: crossing the full face shifted the eye's translate by a few tenths of a
	   pixel) — the eyes read as frozen exactly when the reader is closest to them, which is the
	   worst place for this to be true. logo-pointer is the same rect-relative-ratio math as the
	   built-in pointer-local plugin (clientX/Y against the bound element's own
	   getBoundingClientRect(), unclamped, plus an "inside" boolean for whether the raw ratio
	   landed in [0,1]) but under different property names (--live-logo-pointer-x/-y-ratio,
	   --live-logo-pointer-inside) so it can coexist with, rather than shadow, the viewport-wide
	   one — the built-in plugin's local names are hardcoded, so a second instance bound to the
	   mark would otherwise overwrite the same inherited properties instead of adding a distinct
	   pair logo.css can blend against. logo.css uses --live-logo-pointer-inside to blend this
	   face-relative ratio in only while the cursor is actually over the mark, and the
	   viewport-wide ratio otherwise — see logo.css for the blend itself.
	*/
}
<div
	aria-hidden="true"
	class="pointer-events-none fixed inset-0"
	data-props-for="pointer-local"
	data-props-to="body"
>
</div>

<script>
	import { propsFor, register } from "prop-for-that";
	import "prop-for-that/auto";

	const IDLE_MS = 3000;

	register({
		key: "pointer-idle",
		scope: "global",
		start(ctx) {
			let timer;
			// Starts idle (matches page load, before any pointermove has ever happened) --
			// only a real pointermove should ever flip this to "active".
			ctx.write("idle", 1);
			const onMove = () => {
				ctx.write("idle", 0);
				clearTimeout(timer);
				timer = setTimeout(() => ctx.write("idle", 1), IDLE_MS);
			};
			window.addEventListener("pointermove", onMove, { passive: true });
			return () => {
				clearTimeout(timer);
				window.removeEventListener("pointermove", onMove);
			};
		},
	});
	propsFor(["pointer-idle"]);

	// Face-relative pointer ratio, bound to the logo mark itself rather than the viewport --
	// see point 4 in the comment above for why the viewport-wide pointer-local isn't enough on
	// its own. Mirrors pointer-local's own math (not reused directly: its local property names
	// are hardcoded, so a second binding to the mark would overwrite rather than add to the
	// inherited viewport-wide values logo.css also needs).
	register({
		key: "logo-pointer",
		scope: "element",
		start(ctx) {
			const el = ctx.target;
			const onMove = (event: PointerEvent) => {
				const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();
				const x = rect.width > 0 ? (event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width : 0;
				const y = rect.height > 0 ? (event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height : 0;
				const inside = x >= 0 && x <= 1 && y >= 0 && y <= 1;
				ctx.write("logo-pointer-x-ratio", x);
				ctx.write("logo-pointer-y-ratio", y);
				ctx.write("logo-pointer-inside", inside ? 1 : 0);
			};
			// Seeded centered (not pointer-local's own 0/0/outside seed) so logo.css's blend
			// starts from the same "centered" default its var() fallbacks already assume.
			ctx.write("logo-pointer-x-ratio", 0.5);
			ctx.write("logo-pointer-y-ratio", 0.5);
			ctx.write("logo-pointer-inside", 0);
			window.addEventListener("pointermove", onMove, { passive: true });
			return () => window.removeEventListener("pointermove", onMove);
		},
	});
	const logoMarkEl = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".logo-mark");
	if (logoMarkEl) propsFor(logoMarkEl, ["logo-pointer"]);
</script>
